body:
https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#the-figure-element

About `figure` element, the spec says:

> The element can thus be used to annotate illustrations, diagrams, photos, code listings, etc.

And it also includes an example:

> This example shows the figure element to mark up a code listing.
> 
> &lt;p&gt;In &lt;a href=​"#l4"&gt;listing 4&lt;/a&gt; we see the primary core interface
> API declaration.&lt;/p&gt;
> &lt;figure id=​"l4"&gt;
>  &lt;figcaption&gt;Listing 4. The primary core interface API declaration.&lt;/figcaption&gt;
>  &lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;interface PrimaryCore {
>  boolean verifyDataLine();
>  void sendData(in sequence&amp;lt;byte&gt; data);
>  void initSelfDestruct();
> }&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
> &lt;/figure&gt;
> &lt;p&gt;The API is designed to use UTF-8.&lt;/p&gt;

The thing is, the description for `figure` is:

> The figure element represents some flow content, optionally with a caption, that is self-contained (like a complete sentence) and is typically referenced as a single unit from the main flow of the document.

I think the example is wrong because the `figure` element is not self-contained and can't be moved in the document flow without losing the meaning.

This paragraph also breaks the example:

> When a figure is referred to from the main content of the document by identifying it by its caption (e.g. by figure number), it enables such content to be easily moved away from that primary content, e.g. to the side of the page, to dedicated pages, or to an appendix, without affecting the flow of the document.

If you move the `figure` element here, the paragraph, link, etc, will look weird.

As another example, a documentation page, like the typical API docs, uses headings, descriptions and the corresponding piece of code for every method. If we wrap the code examples in `figure`, means that we can move those self-contained units away from primary content, and that's not true.

For completeness, the w3c specs says this: (sorry if its a bad practice to cite w3c spec here)

> The figure element represents a unit of content, optionally with a caption, that is self-contained, that is typically referenced as a single unit from the main flow of the document, and that can be moved away from the main flow of the document without affecting the document’s meaning.

So again, a `figure` element is not perfect fit for `code` snippets because you can't move them away from the main flow.

I can't think of examples that could apply here because most of code snippets comes with paragraph explanations. Maybe wrapping something like jsfiddle editor panels? Don't know. But i think the example in the spec is not valid.

As a note, the spec also says:

> Self-contained in this context does not necessarily mean independent. For example, each sentence in a paragraph is self-contained; an image that is part of a sentence would be inappropriate for figure, but an entire sentence made of images would be fitting.

That confuses me, i can't really understand things like "an entire sentence made of images". Emojis? ... what? :sweat_smile:

So... as a final question... what's the use case of `figure` with `code` inside? Apparently articles, documentation pages, and those things, can't make pieces of code as "self-contained units".
